-- title:
--   Definitions for Fast and efficient exact synthesis of single qubit unitaries generated by Clifford and T gates
-- statement:
--   The objects the formalized statements of *Fast and efficient exact synthesis of single qubit unitaries generated by Clifford and T gates* (arXiv:1206.5236) are stated in. Each was checked against the paper by a blind read-back and a moderator.
--
--   - `synthesisRing`: The actual complex subring Z[1/sqrt(2),i], PDF pp.2,6.
--   - `integralRing`: The cyclotomic integer subring Z[omega], PDF p.6.
--   - `omegaInteger`: Omega as a cyclotomic integer, PDF p.6.
--   - `sqrtTwoInteger`: sqrt(2) = omega - omega^3 in Z[omega], PDF p.20, equation (15).
--   - `IsSDE`: Finite least-exponent graph, Definition 1, PDF p.6. Signed integer exponents are retained; zero has no finite least exponent because every exponent clears it.
--   - `gde`: Greatest dividing exponent in Z[omega], Definition 2, PDF p.6. Outside Z[omega] this helper returns infinity; every theorem uses it only on cyclotomic integers (including squared norms of such integers).
--   - `gdeTwo`: Same valuation with explicit base 2, PDF pp.20-21.
--   - `normSquared`: Squared ordinary complex modulus, embedded back into C, PDF p.6.
--   - `DividesIntegral`: Divisibility with quotient in Z[omega], never divisibility in the field C, PDF p.6. The bases occurring below are sqrt(2) and 2.
--   - `halfValuation`: Floor of half an extended natural valuation, PDF p.10. Infinity stays infinity.
--   - `coordinateValue`: The ordered omega-power coordinates of a cyclotomic integer, PDF pp.6,12.
--   - `formP`: Quadratic form P, PDF p.12, equation (12).
--   - `formQ`: Quadratic form Q, PDF p.12, equation (13).
--   - `HTLetter`: Single-qubit instructions of Theorem 1, PDF p.2. No ancillary wire or phase letter.
--   - `htMatrix`: Exact matrices from PDF pp.1-2.
--   - `htWordMatrix`: Chronological H/T words: the head acts first, PDF pp.2,5.
--   - `EntriesInRing`: Membership of every matrix entry in the synthesis ring, PDF p.2.
--   - `cliffordTLibrary`: The multi-qubit Clifford/T generators named on PDF pp.1,3. GateApp supplies an ordered tuple of distinct wires, so either CNOT orientation is allowed.
--   - `controlledT`: Controlled-T in the shared two-wire register convention, PDF p.3.
--   - `phaseCompletion`: The unitary completion displayed on PDF pp.4-5.
--   - `wordsAt`: Breadth-first H/T word level, PDF p.7. This enumerates words, not a table whose correctness is supplied as a premise.
--   - `baseDepth`: First BFS level containing a matrix, PDF pp.7-8. The fallback 0 is used only when no exact word exists. Synthesis correctness establishes that this fallback is never used on a valid base-table input.
--   - `baseWord`: BFS lookup at its first successful level, PDF pp.7-8. Restricting this function to height at most 3 is the fixed small-height table; its existence and a uniform finite BFS cutoff are conclusions of algorithm1_correct, not assumptions.
--   - `finiteExponent`: Finite signed exponent when it exists, PDF p.6. The fallback 0 is deliberately not used in the valuation lemmas, which state the least-exponent graph directly.
--   - `matrixHeight`: Squared-entry matrix height of PDF p.7. A zero upper-left entry is the explicit monomial special case and has height 0, without taking sde(0).
--   - `reduction`: One reduction, PDF pp.7-8: V becomes H T^(-k) V.
--   - `reductionChoice`: Guarded search through k=0,1,2,3 once each, PDF pp.7-8.
--   - `synthesisFuel`: Finite recursion implementing the intended Algorithm 1, PDF pp.7-8. Printed: an inner `while state=unfound` repeats an unchanged failing test. Changed: one guarded test per k, as the surrounding p.7 proof requires. The chronological suffix [H,T,...,T] reconstructs the right inverse of H T^(-k); residual output therefore precedes that suffix. Exhausted fuel or no reducing k falls back to BFS; these branches are unreachable above height 3 on a valid input started with its height as fuel. This repair needs independent moderation.
--   - `algorithm1`: Algorithm 1 with enough fuel for every height reduction, PDF pp.7-8. Exact equality/comparison on the algebraic input is modeled classically here; this interface makes no claim about the cost of operations on encodings.
--   - `SynthesisTrace`: A finite execution of the intended Algorithm 1, PDF pp.7-8. Base acceptance is the result of the actual BFS filter being nonempty; its truth is not an input assumption of the synthesis theorem. Every reduction follows the guarded search, and one input-independent bound B suffices for all base lookups.
